1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,043
The Science Behind Cannabis.

2
00:00:02,043 --> 00:00:04,581
Episode four: The body's own cannabinoids.

3
00:00:04,581 --> 00:00:07,362
The brain does not make receptors for a plant.

4
00:00:07,362 --> 00:00:11,956
If a receptor exists, the body must produce its own molecule to activate it.

5
00:00:11,956 --> 00:00:16,368
This episode tells how those molecules, the endocannabinoids, were found.

6
00:00:17,868 --> 00:00:20,445
The logic came from an earlier discovery.

7
00:00:20,445 --> 00:00:23,902
In 1973, opioid receptors were identified in the brain,

8
00:00:23,902 --> 00:00:27,295
and two years later researchers found the enkephalins,

9
00:00:27,295 --> 00:00:31,192
small peptides made by the body that activate those receptors.

10
00:00:31,192 --> 00:00:34,145
Morphine, from the poppy, simply mimicked them.

11
00:00:34,145 --> 00:00:39,613
By analogy, once the cannabinoid receptor was characterised in 1988 and cloned in 1990,

12
00:00:39,613 --> 00:00:45,206
the expectation was clear: somewhere in the body there must be an endogenous cannabinoid,

13
00:00:45,206 --> 00:00:48,160
a natural ligand, and THC must be imitating it.

14
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:52,182
The search began in Raphael Mechoulam's laboratory in Jerusalem.

15
00:00:53,682 --> 00:00:55,473
The team made a key assumption.

16
00:00:55,473 --> 00:01:01,021
Since the receptor binds very lipophilic molecules, the natural ligand was probably a lipid too,

17
00:01:01,021 --> 00:01:04,777
not a peptide or an amino acid like most known neurotransmitters.

18
00:01:04,777 --> 00:01:06,916
So instead of water-soluble extracts,

19
00:01:06,916 --> 00:01:11,192
William Devane and Lumír Hanuš extracted the fatty fractions of pig brain.

20
00:01:11,192 --> 00:01:16,913
To find the needle in the haystack, they used a binding assay: a radioactive synthetic cannabinoid,

21
00:01:16,913 --> 00:01:19,456
HU-243, was allowed to bind to the receptor,

22
00:01:19,456 --> 00:01:23,270
and each brain fraction was tested for its ability to displace it.

23
00:01:23,270 --> 00:01:28,471
Fractions that displaced the radioligand contained something that recognised the receptor.

24
00:01:29,971 --> 00:01:34,458
In 1992, the group reported the first endocannabinoid in the journal Science.

25
00:01:34,458 --> 00:01:40,285
The molecule was N-arachidonoylethanolamine: a long, unsaturated fatty acid called arachidonic acid,

26
00:01:40,285 --> 00:01:42,732
joined to a small ethanolamine head group.

27
00:01:42,732 --> 00:01:48,267
It looked nothing like THC, yet it bound the receptor and produced the same cellular responses.

28
00:01:48,267 --> 00:01:54,502
Mechoulam named it anandamide, from the Sanskrit word ananda, meaning bliss, and the chemical ending amide.

29
00:01:54,502 --> 00:01:58,755
Anandamide turned out to be a partial agonist: it activates the receptor,

30
00:01:58,755 --> 00:02:01,028
but less fully than some other ligands,

31
00:02:01,028 --> 00:02:04,465
and it is present in the brain at quite low concentrations.

32
00:02:05,965 --> 00:02:08,695
A second endocannabinoid followed in 1995.

33
00:02:08,695 --> 00:02:16,298
Working with Shimon Ben-Shabat and Hanuš, Mechoulam's group isolated two-arachidonoylglycerol, 2-AG, from canine gut,

34
00:02:16,298 --> 00:02:22,537
and in the same year Takayuki Sugiura's group in Japan independently identified it in rat brain.

35
00:02:22,537 --> 00:02:27,476
Two A G is also built from arachidonic acid, this time attached to glycerol,

36
00:02:27,476 --> 00:02:29,426
the backbone of ordinary fats.

37
00:02:29,426 --> 00:02:33,455
Unlike anandamide, 2-AG is a full agonist at both CB1 and CB2,

38
00:02:33,455 --> 00:02:36,120
and it is far more abundant in the brain,

39
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:38,719
by roughly a hundred to a thousand fold.

40
00:02:38,719 --> 00:02:44,178
Many researchers now regard 2-AG as the main signalling endocannabinoid at synapses.

41
00:02:45,678 --> 00:02:50,394
The two endocannabinoids share a common origin: both are derived from arachidonic acid,

42
00:02:50,394 --> 00:02:53,538
a fatty acid that is a normal component of cell membranes.

43
00:02:53,538 --> 00:02:59,230
They differ in the head group, ethanolamine versus glycerol, in how strongly they activate the receptors,

44
00:02:59,230 --> 00:03:01,561
and in how much of each the brain contains.

45
00:03:01,561 --> 00:03:04,922
They also differ in the enzymes that make and break them down,

46
00:03:04,922 --> 00:03:06,982
which we will see in the next episode.

47
00:03:06,982 --> 00:03:10,234
Both molecules are also found outside the brain, in the gut,

48
00:03:10,234 --> 00:03:15,817
the immune system and many other tissues, reflecting the wide distribution of the receptors themselves.

49
00:03:17,317 --> 00:03:21,020
Endocannabinoids break the textbook rules for neurotransmitters.

50
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:26,863
Classical transmitters such as glutamate or dopamine are made in advance, packed into small vesicles,

51
00:03:26,863 --> 00:03:29,292
and released when a nerve impulse arrives.

52
00:03:29,292 --> 00:03:31,549
Endocannabinoids are not stored at all.

53
00:03:31,549 --> 00:03:36,292
They are produced on demand, cut out of membrane lipids when a cell is stimulated,

54
00:03:36,292 --> 00:03:40,920
released immediately, act on nearby receptors, and are then quickly broken down.

55
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:48,904
Several other lipids, including noladin ether, virodhamine and N-arachidonoyl dopamine, have been proposed as additional endocannabinoids,

56
00:03:48,904 --> 00:03:53,416
but their roles are less firmly established than those of anandamide and 2-AG.

57
00:03:54,916 --> 00:03:59,739
In the final episode, we put the pieces together: how endocannabinoids are made and removed,

58
00:03:59,739 --> 00:04:02,675
and how they carry signals backwards across the synapse.
